#!/usr/bin/python3
"""lp-install - put the running LP system onto another disk, for good.

The USB stick this OS is written to boots a real, disk-rooted system: ext4
on the stick, read-write, nothing in RAM. Installing is therefore not
"unpack an archive somewhere" but "make another copy of exactly this
system on the laptop's NVMe and make the firmware boot it". This program
does that, and the installer window (lp-installer) is a face on it: every
decision and every write is here, so the same install can be run from a
serial console, from a script in a test, or by a person at a terminal.

    lp-install list  [--json]        the disks, their partitions and free
                                     space, and which could take LP
    lp-install check [--json]        firmware facts that stop an install
    lp-install install --disk /dev/nvme0n1 [--yes] [--progress]
                       [--lang ko_KR.UTF-8] [--no-boot-entry]
                       [--user NAME --fullname "Full Name" --password-stdin]
                       [--hostname NAME] [--timezone Asia/Seoul]
    lp-install install --partition /dev/nvme0n1p3 [same options]
    lp-install install --free-space /dev/nvme0n1 [--size MiB] [--no-recovery]
                       [same options]
    lp-install install ... --dry-run [--json]
                                     what would be erased, made and kept,
                                     partition by partition; nothing written

What an install writes (the disk layout contract, COMMON.md):

    --disk        the whole disk, erased:
    GPT   1  LP-ESP       FAT32   512 MiB   the kernel, the boot menu
          2  LP-RECOVERY  ext4    ~1.3 GiB  the recovery system (sized to it)
          3  LP-ROOT      ext4      rest    this system, copied

    --partition   one partition of a GPT disk, erased; the rest of the
                  disk (Windows, somebody's data) left as it is:
          n  LP-ROOT      ext4    as it was this system, copied; the
                                            partition keeps its PARTUUID
          +  the disk's own EFI System Partition gets \\EFI\\LP\\ - no
             other file on it is touched; a disk that has none gets
             LP-ESP (FAT32, 512 MiB) in its free space, or is refused

    --free-space  new partitions in the disk's largest unallocated
                  region; nothing that is there now is erased:
          +  LP-ESP       FAT32   512 MiB   LP's own start-up partition
          +  LP-RECOVERY  ext4    ~1.3 GiB  the recovery system
          +  LP-ROOT      ext4      rest    this system, copied
                  when the region holds all three (free_layout says why
                  recovery brings its own LP-ESP); when it does not, LP-ROOT
                  alone, started from the disk's EFI partition as a
                  partition install is (with a new LP-ESP in front of it
                  when the disk has none, or its EFI partition is full,
                  unreadable, or already starts another LP)

    On a disk that already carries LP's partition names (another LP
    there), what an install names is LP-ROOT-2 and so on, and a
    free-space install makes no LP-RECOVERY: the recovery system finds
    its partitions by name, and must not find two.

    A partition install makes no LP-RECOVERY: there is no room that is
    LP's to take for it, and the boot menu offers Recovery only on a
    disk that has one (\\EFI\\LP\\recovery.ok). Every install names its root
    by PARTUUID in \\EFI\\LP\\cmdline.txt and its recovery by PARTUUID in
    recovery.ok, so a disk with several LP partitions, or a stick with
    the same labels plugged in beside it, boots the one installed.

Why it is built the way it is:

  - It refuses the disk it is running from, and any disk with anything
    mounted, swapped on or held by device-mapper. Those are the two ways
    an installer destroys the system it is running on, and both can be
    told from /proc and /sys before a single byte is written. The one
    exception erases nothing: when the disk LP runs from is big enough
    (a VM's disk made bigger than the image), LP is installed where it
    is - its table rewritten for the disk, LP-ROOT grown, the answers
    written in (install_inplace).

  - The root is copied from the running system with rsync -aHAX: owners
    and modes, hard links (a Debian root has hundreds), ACLs, extended
    attributes (file capabilities live there - ping loses its right to
    open a raw socket without them), symlinks and device nodes. -x keeps
    it on the root filesystem, so /proc, /sys, /dev, /tmp and the stick's
    own EFI partition are not walked into.

  - Every partition gets a fresh GUID (sfdisk makes them), so the
    installed root's PARTUUID is its own and preinit can tell it from
    the stick's when both are plugged in - which is the normal state of
    affairs at the first reboot.

  - The firmware boot entry is written straight into efivarfs rather than
    through efibootmgr: it is forty lines of struct packing, and a tool
    that is not on the stick is a boot entry that is not written. The
    removable-media path \\EFI\\BOOT\\BOOTX64.EFI is always written too,
    because firmware forgets its entries (a BIOS update, a CMOS reset)
    and a disk that boots only through NVRAM does not survive that.

  - The copy is made to look like a machine nobody has used yet: no
    machine-id, no SSH host key, no installer marker. /etc/rc makes the
    first two on the first boot; the first-boot setup (lp-firstboot)
    runs once because the installer leaves /etc/lp/firstboot behind.

  - The person's account is made here, in the copy, when the window
    asked for it (--user, password on stdin): the stick's live account
    (uid 1000, "user", no usable password) becomes theirs, with their
    name, their password as a SHA-512 crypt hash, and group sudo; root
    is locked. The same hash goes onto LP-RECOVERY as the recovery
    password (/etc/lp/recovery-password, 0600), so the recovery shell
    can still ask for it when LP-ROOT is too damaged to read. What was
    answered here is recorded in /etc/lp/firstboot, and the first-boot
    setup asks only the rest. The password is never on a command line,
    where every process on the machine could read it from /proc.

  - Installing into a partition shares the disk with another system, so
    everything that belongs to the disk is read, never rewritten: the
    partition table only gets the chosen partition's name (LP-ROOT) and
    type changed, or one partition added in space nobody uses; the EFI
    partition is the disk's own, and LP writes only under \\EFI\\LP\\ -
    the fallback \\EFI\\BOOT\\BOOTX64.EFI only when there is none, because
    on a Windows disk that file is how the firmware finds Windows once
    its NVRAM entry is gone. What is refused is refused before a byte is
    written: an MBR disk (the EFI boot path needs GPT), a partition
    mounted, swapped on or held, the EFI partition itself, one smaller
    than a copy needs, an EFI partition without the room for the kernel.

  - Two firmware settings on the Dell XPS 15 9550 stop an install before
    it starts, and neither can be changed from here: Secure Boot (the
    kernel is not signed by Microsoft, so an installed copy would not
    start) and SATA Operation = RAID On (the NVMe is behind Intel RST and
    Linux does not see it at all). Both are detected and explained.

Every Debian tool is called by its /usr path. /bin on this system is our
own userland (mount, blkid, sync ... are ours there and take different
options), and the tools below are the Debian ones this program was
written against.
"""

